17:T67a,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Are Canadian scholarships automatically considered with admission?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Some are, but many are not. Major awards may require a separate form, early admission or school nomination. Read the official award page and the department's funding instructions."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Does a funded PhD offer cover the full cost of living in Canada?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not necessarily. Compare the net amount after tuition and fees with housing, food, transport and insurance in the specific city, and check whether part of the package requires paid work."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do Canadian scholarships use one national application system?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. University awards, research funding and external scholarships can have different application routes. Check the specific institution and award owner."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can an undergraduate scholarship require school nomination?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Some major awards, including the University of Toronto Pearson route, use a school nomination before later admission and scholarship steps. Follow the current official sequence."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How should I evaluate a graduate funding offer in Canada?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Ask for the guaranteed amount, duration, tuition and compulsory fees, assistantship duties, renewal conditions and what remains after charges. Then compare the net amount with costs in the actual city."}}]}1:["$","main",null,{"id":"main-content","className":"editorial-page editorial-page--country","children":[[["$","script","0",{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"WebPage\",\"@id\":\"https://aspirecompass.org/countries/canada/\",\"name\":\"Scholarships in C